# JIT_DISABLE
# reason: the interpreter's own recovery paths are what is under test; inside
# compiled code both errors still end the program (see the note below)
#~
Try() -- and '?->', which is Try() on the chain -- recovers from an invalid
object cast and from a full call stack, as it does from every other runtime
error.

Both used to end the program even inside a guarded chain (#900). ObjInstCast
printed its message and exited without consulting the handler stack, and
PushFrame's overflow path was a bare exit(1). Recovery pops frames back to the
handler's depth, which is also what frees the call stack again after a runaway
recursion.

The errors here are raised deep inside a called method rather than in the chain
expression itself, which is the case that matters: the handler is far below the
frame that fails.

Unguarded, both still print their message and exit 1; bad_runtime_stack.obs
covers the unguarded overflow.

This test opts out of the JIT pass, because the fix reaches only what the
interpreter owns. Inside compiled code both errors still end the program: a
failing cast is raised in a JIT callback, which has no way to reach this
interpreter's handler stack, and compiled recursion overruns the native C stack
before any frame-count guard can fire. A compiled callee that reports a guard
stub's status does recover, since its frame has already returned.
~#

class Animal { New() {} }
class Dog from Animal { New() { Parent(); } }
class Cat from Animal { New() { Parent(); } }

class Holder {
	@animal : Animal;

	New(animal : Animal) {
		@animal := animal;
	}

	# fails: the animal is a Cat
	method : public : AsDog() ~ Dog {
		return @animal->As(Dog);
	}

	# runs the call stack out. The work after the call keeps the compiler's
	# tail-call pass from rewriting this into a loop, which would spin forever
	# instead of overflowing (the same trap bad_runtime_stack.obs names).
	method : public : Deep(n : Int) ~ Dog {
		deeper := Deep(n + 1);
		if(deeper = Nil) {
			return Nil;
		};

		return deeper;
	}
}

class TryRecoversCastAndDepth {
	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		ok := true;

		cat_holder := Holder->New(Cat->New());

		bad_cast := cat_holder?->AsDog();
		ok := Report("invalid cast in a guarded chain yields Nil", bad_cast = Nil) & ok;

		deep := cat_holder?->Deep(0);
		ok := Report("full call stack in a guarded chain yields Nil", deep = Nil) & ok;

		# the program carries on, and the call stack is usable again
		dog_holder := Holder->New(Dog->New());
		good := dog_holder?->AsDog();
		ok := Report("a valid cast after recovery returns its object", good <> Nil) & ok;

		if(<>ok) {
			Runtime->Exit(1);
		};
	}

	function : Report(name : String, ok : Bool) ~ Bool {
		if(ok) {
			"PASS: {$name}"->PrintLine();
			return true;
		};

		"FAIL: {$name}"->PrintLine();
		return false;
	}
}
